{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And it was about an ephah of barley.</b> Once a man asked me, what is the reason for 'an ephah of barley'? I said to him, there is no reason to this question, for Scripture merely relates what was. Then I was slight in his eyes, and he said to me that he has a reason, and I was silent and did not ask him. And behold, he came one day and said to me that he has many reasons." "<b>The first:</b> that Ruth saw by way of prophecy that one of her sons was destined to establish a pillar in her husband's name, and the meaning of 'איפה' — ninety-six pomegranates that were on the pillar, and this is numerically 'איפה', and the meaning of 'שעורים' is like 'שיעורים' (measures), like 'שער בנפשו' (he determined in his soul). Another reason: 'ויהי' — two words, Aramaic and Hebrew. And it is when she saw that Absalom, who is of her sons, would be hanged, and behold כ with א alludes to the years of Absalom at his death. And afterward 'איפה שערים' — plural form, because it was heavy upon him. Another reason: that the first letter of כאיפה together with the first of שעורים is numerically 'ישי', who is a completely righteous man. Then he rejoiced and his heart was at ease within him."], :book :ruth, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {1 "'כ with א' likely means the combined numerical value of the letters כ (20) and א (1); 'the years of Absalom at his death' possibly refers to the episode in 2 Samuel 14:26–27, though the exact calculation is left opaque as Ibn Ezra reports it."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Ruth -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/ruth"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:43:42.921913273Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}